{
  "id": "k8s-rbac",
  "provider": "kubernetes",
  "title": "Kubernetes RBAC: Role, ClusterRole and bindings",
  "summary": "Bindings connect subjects to role definitions. The binding's kind sets where the permissions apply, not the role's kind.",
  "notes": [
    "A RoleBinding that references a ClusterRole grants that ClusterRole's permissions only inside the binding's namespace. This is how built-in roles like 'edit' are reused per namespace.",
    "A ClusterRoleBinding grants cluster-wide, across all namespaces. Keep these rare and review them first.",
    "RBAC is additive: there are no deny rules. Least privilege means granting less, not subtracting.",
    "Set automountServiceAccountToken: false on pods that don't call the Kubernetes API."
  ],
  "docs": [
    {
      "title": "Using RBAC authorization",
      "url": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/rbac/"
    },
    {
      "title": "RBAC good practices",
      "url": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/rbac-good-practices/"
    },
    {
      "title": "Service accounts",
      "url": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/service-accounts/"
    }
  ],
